From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:00:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: room in the pool before kept prompts go,
 TF_GLM_CACHE_ROOM (default off)

On the AFD attention node the --parallel pool is one RTX 5090's: 626,688 tokens at TF_GLM_CACHE_GIB=8, against about
2.9M in the recipe's two-Spark lane. A few 65K-100K contexts fill it, and its free rows end up in pieces between
extents. MultiDecoder._room and _grow (MiaAI-Lab recipe patch 0030, unchanged at v1.5) evict kept prompts, least
recently used first, until a gap fits, and move the extents together only once nothing is left to evict. In
llama-benchy's long cells they evicted the contexts that queued inference requests were about to resume while the
free rows already covered the need, and each such miss refilled its whole context and evicted the next one.

- cache_room.py (new). With TF_GLM_CACHE_ROOM=1, room() takes a new extent's rows from the free rows when they cover
  it, moving the extents together (the ported _compact) when no gap fits, and evicts only while the free rows fall
  short. grow() grows an extent in place or moves it to a gap as before; else it evicts only what the free rows lack
  and packs the extents with the rows the extent needs right after it. Each eviction takes, in the ported _victims
  order, a kept prompt no waiting request would resume first, and the one the latest waiting request wants last.
- multi.py: _room and _grow hand over to cache_room when the switch is on (rank 0; rank 1 applies EVICT, MOVE and GROW
  as before, with no setting of its own). GlmScheduler gives the decoder its waiting prompts. /health gains cache_room
  (moves, moved rows, evictions, evictions of a wanted prompt).
- engine.py: a startup line when the switch is on.

Unset, _room and _grow run the recipe's code; replies, prompt chunks and kept points do not change either way, only
which kept prompts survive and where the extents sit.

Tests: tests/test_glm_cache_room.py, on two ranks with every reply checked against the request served alone. A new
prompt with the free rows in pieces: off, the two oldest kept prompts go; on, the extents move and every kept prompt
stays and resumes. A resumed prompt that must grow into its neighbour's block: off, the neighbour's prompt goes; on,
the neighbours move. Evictions take what no waiting request resumes first, then the latest wanted. Growing streams
keep every row through the moves, both settings. llama-benchy's runs in a scaled-down pool (13 blocks, 2 streams, 3
contexts a run): off, 3 of 12 inference requests miss; on, none. The scheduler's waiting prompts, held one first.

A CPU replay of llama-benchy's three long arena cells at the 5090's pool (306 blocks, 4 streams, 3 runs each, the fake
model):
d100000_c5 resumes 12 of 15 contexts off and 15 of 15 on (39.6% and 49.5% of prompt tokens cached, 49.5% possible);
d65535_c10 resumes 0 of 30 off and 24 of 30 on (0.0% and 39.4%); d32768_c10 resumes 30 of 30 both ways. In
d65535_c10, ten contexts need 320 blocks, so at least one a run must go.

Credit: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM-5.3-Flash CUDA concurrent streams") records the change to the ported _room and
_grow.

